# Bonus — GitLab Integration

This bonus adds a local GitLab instance to the cluster built in Part 3,
and reconfigures Argo CD to sync from it instead of GitHub.

## What's included

- `confs/gitlab-values.yaml` — Helm values used to install GitLab
- `confs/gitlab-app.yaml` — Argo CD Application pointing at the local GitLab repo
- `scripts/install_gitlab.sh` — installs GitLab into the `gitlab` namespace

## Architecture

GitLab runs inside the same k3s cluster as Argo CD, in its own `gitlab`
namespace, installed via the official GitLab Helm chart. Argo CD reaches
it through the in-cluster service DNS name — no external ingress or DNS
needed.

- GitLab chart version: `9.10.5` (app version v18.10.5) — the latest
  chart version that still bundles PostgreSQL, Redis, and MinIO. Newer
  chart versions require external database services, which adds
  unnecessary complexity for a local single-node lab cluster.

## 1. Install GitLab

```bash
kubectl create namespace gitlab
./bonus/scripts/install_gitlab.sh
```

This installs GitLab via Helm and waits for pods to come up
(takes 10–15 minutes on first install).

## 2. Access the GitLab UI (for setup only)

```bash
kubectl port-forward -n gitlab svc/gitlab-webservice-default 8181:8181
```

Then browse to `http://localhost:8181`.

Get the root password:

```bash
kubectl get secret gitlab-gitlab-initial-root-password -n gitlab \
  -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d; echo
```

Login: `root` / (password above)

## 3. Create a project and push manifests

1. In the GitLab UI, create a new blank project (e.g. `p3_bonus`)
2. Create a Personal Access Token: user menu → Edit profile →
   Access Tokens → scopes `api` + `write_repository`
3. Push the Part 3 manifests to it:

```bash
cd p3/dev   # wherever the Part 3 manifests live
git remote add gitlab http://root:<TOKEN>@localhost:8181/root/p3_bonus.git
git push gitlab main
```

## 4. Point Argo CD at the local GitLab repo

Apply the provided Application manifest:

```bash
kubectl apply -f bonus/confs/gitlab-app.yaml
```

This Application points to:

http://gitlab-webservice-default.gitlab.svc.cluster.local:8181/root/p3_bonus.git


using the in-cluster DNS name, since Argo CD and GitLab share the
same cluster network.

If the repo is private, register credentials with Argo CD:

```bash
argocd repo add http://gitlab-webservice-default.gitlab.svc.cluster.local:8181/root/p3_bonus.git \
  --username root \
  --password <TOKEN>
```

## 5. Verify

```bash
kubectl get applications -n argocd
```

Should show `Synced` / `Healthy`. Pushing a change to the GitLab
project triggers Argo CD to sync it into the `dev` namespace, exactly
as it did with GitHub in Part 3.
